en.note:
# String internals

**What it is.** `System.String` is a sealed reference type whose instances are immutable: once constructed, the character data never changes. Every API that looks like a mutation (`Replace`, `ToUpperInvariant`, `Substring`, `Trim`, `+`) returns a new instance. The object layout is: object header, method-table pointer, an `int` length, then the UTF-16 code units stored inline, followed by a hidden null terminator kept for interop. On x64 that is about `22 + 2 * Length` bytes rounded up to a multiple of 8 (an empty string is 24 bytes). Because the characters live inside the object, a string is a variable-length object: there is no safe-code way to mutate the characters after construction, and the only safe way to fill a string in place is `string.Create`, which hands you the buffer before the instance is published. Strings longer than roughly 42,500 chars exceed 85,000 bytes and land on the Large Object Heap.

**UTF-16.** `string` is always UTF-16; there is no UTF-8 string type in the BCL (`Utf8String` was prototyped and shelved). `Length` and the indexer work in `char` units (UTF-16 code units), not Unicode scalar values and not user-perceived characters. Anything outside the Basic Multilingual Plane, including most emoji, is a surrogate pair of two `char`s. Use `Rune` and `EnumerateRunes()` (.NET Core 3.0) for scalar values, `StringInfo.GetTextElementEnumerator` / `StringInfo.GetNextTextElementLength` for grapheme clusters (UAX#29-compliant since .NET 5), and `Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount` for wire size. A naive `Reverse()` or slicing by `char` index can split a pair and produce invalid text. Since C# 11, `"..."u8` literals give you a `ReadOnlySpan<byte>` of UTF-8 without any `string` at all.

**Equality and hashing.** `==` and `Equals` on `string` are ordinal value comparisons (with a reference-equality fast path); only `ReferenceEquals` or comparing as `object` tests identity. `GetHashCode()` is randomized per process on .NET Core and later (Marvin), so never persist it or share it across processes. `Compare`, `StartsWith(string)`, `EndsWith(string)` and `IndexOf(string)` are culture-sensitive by default, while `Equals` and `IndexOf(char)` are ordinal; pass `StringComparison.Ordinal` in non-UI code.

**Interning.** The C# compiler folds constant expressions (`"hel" + "lo"`, `const` interpolation since C# 10) into one literal, and the runtime interns every literal (`ldstr`) into a process-wide table, so identical literals across assemblies share a single object. Since .NET 8 literals are allocated on the Frozen Object Heap: they are never moved or collected and the GC does not have to trace them. Runtime-created strings (concatenation, parsing, I/O) are *not* interned. `string.Intern(s)` returns the canonical instance (adding `s` if absent); `string.IsInterned(s)` only looks up. Interned strings live until the process exits, so interning unbounded input such as header values or user ids is a memory leak, and each call costs a hash plus synchronization. Interning is an optimization for identity-keyed caches or heavily duplicated tokens, never a correctness requirement, because `==` already compares by value.

**Building strings.** `s += x` in a loop is O(n^2): each iteration copies everything written so far. A single expression `a + b + c + d` compiles to one `string.Concat(a, b, c, d)` call and is fine. Since .NET Framework 4.0 `StringBuilder` is a backward-linked list of chunks that grow to at most 8,000 chars by default (a single large `Append` or an explicit capacity can create a bigger chunk): `Append` copies nothing until the current chunk is full, `ToString()` copies exactly once, but the indexer and `Insert` may walk the chunk list. Since C# 10 / .NET 6, interpolated strings compile to `DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler`, a `ref struct` that formats directly into an `ArrayPool<char>` buffer, and `sb.Append($"...")` binds to `AppendInterpolatedStringHandler`, so no intermediate string exists. Prefer `AsSpan()` slicing and the `ReadOnlySpan<char>` overloads of `int.Parse`, `string.Concat` and `string.Join` (.NET 9 added `params ReadOnlySpan<T>` overloads) over `Substring`.

**`string.Create`.** When the final length is known up front, `string.Create(length, state, SpanAction<char, TState>)` (.NET Core 2.1) allocates the string once and hands you a `Span<char>` over its still-private buffer to fill: no temporary array and no copy. `new string(ReadOnlySpan<char>)` also allocates once but copies from your buffer. .NET 6 added `string.Create(IFormatProvider, ref DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler)` for culture-specific interpolation.

**What interviewers probe.** Why immutability exists (safe sharing across threads, interning, stable dictionary keys, tamper-proof paths and URIs), reference versus value equality, `Length` on emoji, the cost of `+` in loops versus `StringBuilder`, whether interpolation allocates, and when `string.Create` beats `StringBuilder`.

**Misconceptions.** "Equal strings are always the same object": only literals are. "Interned strings are collected when unused": they are not. "`Length` is the number of characters": it is the number of UTF-16 code units. "`StringBuilder` always beats concatenation": one `Concat` of a handful of operands is cheaper. "Strings cannot be mutated": `fixed (char* p = s)` can overwrite one, which silently corrupts a shared literal; the runtime does not protect you.

```csharp
using System.Text;

string a = "hello";
string b = "hel" + "lo";                                 // folded by the compiler: same interned literal
string c = new string("hello".ToCharArray());            // runtime instance
Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, b));                // True
Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, c));                // False
Console.WriteLine(a == c);                               // True  (ordinal value equality)
Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, string.Intern(c))); // True

string s = "a👍";
Console.WriteLine(s.Length);                             // 3 (UTF-16 code units)
Console.WriteLine(s.EnumerateRunes().Count());           // 2 (scalar values)
Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount(s));        // 5 (bytes on the wire)

// Exactly one heap allocation: the result string itself.
static string GuidToHex(Guid id) =>
    string.Create(32, id, static (dst, g) =>
    {
        Span<byte> bytes = stackalloc byte[16];
        g.TryWriteBytes(bytes);
        const string digits = "0123456789abcdef";
        for (int i = 0; i < bytes.Length; i++)
        {
            dst[2 * i]     = digits[bytes[i] >> 4];
            dst[2 * i + 1] = digits[bytes[i] & 0xF];
        }
    });
```

